NAME
       mount - attach a file system to the directory tree

SYNOPSIS
       mount
       mount -t TYPE [-o OPTIONS] SOURCE TARGET
       mount --bind|--rbind SOURCE TARGET
       mount -o remount[,ro|rw] TARGET
       mount --make-[r]private|shared|slave|unbindable TARGET

DESCRIPTION
       Without arguments, print the mount table of the calling process
       (the contents of /proc/self/mounts). With arguments, mount a file
       system on TARGET, which must be an existing directory. Requires
       root.

       Every mount namespace has its own mount table. A mount made after
       unshare -m is visible only inside that namespace. A mount made over
       a directory that is already a mount point replaces that mount in
       the current namespace; mounts are not stacked.

OPTIONS
       -t, --types TYPE
           tmpfs, proc, sysfs, devtmpfs, kernelfs, vfat (SOURCE is a block
           device such as /dev/sda, its EFI system partition is used) or
           ext2 (only the device that holds the running root file system)

       -o, --options LIST
           comma separated: ro, rw, remount, bind, rbind and the accepted
           but ignored nosuid, nodev, noexec, sync, noatime, relatime and
           similar. Other words are passed to the file system as data and
           are currently ignored

       -r, --read-only
           same as -o ro

       -B, --bind
           make SOURCE visible at TARGET. SOURCE may be a subdirectory of a
           mounted file system. Only directories can be bound

       -R, --rbind
           like --bind, and also copy every mount below SOURCE

       --make-private, --make-rprivate, --make-shared, --make-slave
           accepted and ignored: all mounts are private

NOTES
       The vfat driver is a single instance. A second vfat device cannot be
       mounted while another one is attached, and ext2 cannot be mounted on
       a second device.

EXAMPLES
       sudo mount -t tmpfs tmpfs /mnt
       sudo mount --bind /usr/share /mnt
       sudo mount -o remount,ro /mnt

SEE ALSO
       umount(8), unshare(1), nsenter(1)
